[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Understanding Pessimistic Locking in Hibernate: PESSIMISTIC_READ vs PESSIMISTIC_WRITE"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Concurrency control is a critical aspect of enterprise application development. When multiple transactions attempt to update the same database records simultaneously, data integrity issues such as lost updates, dirty reads, and non-repeatable reads can occur. Hibernate provides two main strategies for concurrency control: optimistic locking and pessimistic locking."},{"order":3,"type":"paragraph","text":"This tutorial focuses on pessimistic locking, specifically examining the differences and use cases for LockModeType.PESSIMISTIC_READ and LockModeType.PESSIMISTIC_WRITE."},{"order":4,"type":"subheading","text":"What is Pessimistic Locking?"},{"order":5,"type":"paragraph","text":"Pessimistic locking is a strategy where you lock the database records at the database level as soon as they are read, and keep the lock until the transaction is completed. This approach assumes that concurrent conflicts are likely to happen, hence it prevents other transactions from modifying or in some cases reading the data until the current transaction releases the lock."},{"order":6,"type":"subheading","text":"PESSIMISTIC_READ"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"PESSIMISTIC_READ forces the database to acquire a shared lock (often implemented as SELECT ... FOR SHARE or SELECT ... LOCK IN SHARE MODE depending on the SQL dialect). This means that the current transaction can read the data and prevent other transactions from modifying or locking it, but other transactions are still allowed to read the data."},{"order":8,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Allows other transactions to read the locked data."},{"order":9,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Prevents other transactions from updating or deleting the locked data."},{"order":10,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Ideal for scenarios requiring repeatable reads where you want to ensure data does not change while you are reading and processing it."},{"order":11,"type":"subheading","text":"PESSIMISTIC_WRITE"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"PESSIMISTIC_WRITE forces the database to acquire an exclusive lock (implemented as SELECT ... FOR UPDATE). This prevents other transactions from reading, modifying, or deleting the locked data until the current transaction is committed or rolled back."},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Prevents other transactions from reading, updating, or deleting the data."},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Provides the highest level of data protection against concurrent modifications."},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Best used when you intend to update the read data within the same transaction."},{"order":16,"type":"subheading","text":"Code Example: Implementing Pessimistic Locks in Hibernate"},{"order":17,"type":"paragraph","text":"To apply a pessimistic lock in Hibernate or JPA, you pass the appropriate LockModeType to the EntityManager find or lock methods. Below is an example demonstrating both modes."},{"order":18,"type":"code","text":"@Service
public class AccountService {

    @Autowired
    private EntityManager entityManager;

    @Transactional
    public Account getAccountForReading(Long accountId) {
        // Acquires a shared lock (PESSIMISTIC_READ)
        return entityManager.find(Account.class, accountId, LockModeType.PESSIMISTIC_READ);
    }

    @Transactional
    public void updateAccountBalance(Long accountId, BigDecimal amount) {
        // Acquires an exclusive lock (PESSIMISTIC_WRITE)
        Account account = entityManager.find(Account.class, accountId, LockModeType.PESSIMISTIC_WRITE);
        account.setBalance(account.getBalance().add(amount));
        entityManager.merge(account);
    }
}"},{"order":19,"type":"subheading","text":"Using Pessimistic Locking with JPQL / Criteria API"},{"order":20,"type":"paragraph","text":"You can also specify lock modes when executing JPQL queries by using the setLockMode method on the TypedQuery interface."},{"order":21,"type":"code","text":"@Transactional
public Product findProductWithWriteLock(Long productId) {
    TypedQuery<Product> query = entityManager.createQuery(
        "SELECT p FROM Product p WHERE p.id = :id", Product.class);
    query.setParameter("id", productId);
    query.setLockMode(LockModeType.PESSIMISTIC_WRITE);
    return query.getSingleResult();
}"},{"order":22,"type":"subheading","text":"Best Practices and Performance Considerations"},{"order":23,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Keep transactions as short as possible to minimize lock contention and avoid database deadlocks."},{"order":24,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use PESSIMISTIC_READ when concurrent updates are rare but you need guaranteed consistency during read-heavy workflows."},{"order":25,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use PESSIMISTIC_WRITE for critical financial transactions or inventory management where strict exclusive access is mandatory."},{"order":26,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Be aware that heavy usage of pessimistic locks can severely impact application scalability under high concurrency."}]
